## 1. Introduction

EntityItems are concrete instances of data that conform to EntityDefinitions. They represent:

- Actual business or configuration data stored in the system
- Instances with specific values for attributes defined in the EntityDefinition
- Time-valid data with specified date intervals
- Potentially references to other entity items

### 1.1 EntityItem Purpose

EntityItems serve to:
- Store actual data values according to a predefined structure
- Maintain validity periods for temporal data tracking
- Establish relationships between business entities
- Provide a standardized data format with built-in system attributes

### 1.2 Relationship to EntityDefinition

- Each EntityItem is associated with one EntityDefinition via `entityDefinitionCode`
- The EntityDefinition determines:
  - What attributes the item can contain
  - Validation rules for those attributes
  - Reference relationships to other entity types
  - Authorization for accessing the item

## 2. Core Structure

Every EntityItem contains these essential system elements:

### 2.1 System Identification

```json
{
  "id": "012345678901234567890123",     // MongoDB ObjectId (generated)
  "awid": "22222222222222222222222222", // Application workspace ID
  "sys": {
    "cts": "2025-01-01T12:00:00.000Z",  // Creation timestamp
    "mts": "2025-01-01T12:00:00.000Z",  // Last modification timestamp
    "rev": 0                            // Revision number
  }
}
```

### 2.2 Definition Reference

```json
{
  "entityDefinitionCode": "accountHolder", // Link to the EntityDefinition
  "code": "ACC-001"                        // Logical entity identifier (business key)
}
```

### 2.3 Temporal Validity

```json
{
  "dateInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01",               // Start of validity (inclusive)
    "to": "2050-12-31"                  // End of validity (inclusive)
  }
}
```

#### 2.3.1 Optional Temporal Elements

For bitemporal model support:

```json
{
  "transactionInterval": {              // When the transaction is valid
    "from": "2023-01-01T12:00:00.000Z",
    "to": "9999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"    // "Forever" in the future
  },
  "validityInterval": {                 // Business validity period
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2050-12-31"
  }
}
```

## 3. Custom Attributes

Beyond system fields, EntityItems contain domain-specific attributes as defined in the EntityDefinition:

### 3.1 Basic Attributes

```json
{
  "name": "Acme Corporation",           // String attribute
  "establishedYear": 1985,              // Number attribute
  "active": true,                       // Boolean attribute
  "registrationDate": "2020-05-15"      // Date attribute
}
```

### 3.2 Attribute Validation

All attributes must conform to the validation rules in the EntityDefinition:

- String attributes must meet length requirements
- Number attributes must be within defined ranges
- Required attributes must be present
- All values must match the defined data types
- Custom validation rules must be satisfied

## 4. Entity References

References to other entities are stored as attribute values:

### 4.1 Simple References

```json
{
  "country": "US",                      // Reference by code
  "type": "CORPORATE"                   // Reference by code
}
```

### 4.2 Artifact References

```json
{
  "issuingBody": "00000000000000000000000000000001", // Reference by artifactId
  "artifactId": "00000000000000000000000000000002"   // OBC reference for this item
}
```

### 4.3 Multiple References

```json
{
  "categories": ["CAT1", "CAT2", "CAT3"]  // Multiple references by code
}
```

## 5. Temporal Data Management

### 5.1 Basic Validity Period

All entity items must have a validity period:

```json
{
  "dateInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01",  // First day of validity
    "to": "2050-12-31"     // Last day of validity
  }
}
```

### 5.2 Overlapping Validity Strategies

When creating or updating items, different strategies for handling overlapping items:

- `overlappingOpen`: Allow overlapping periods
- `overlappingClosed`: Allow overlapping with open/closed intervals
- `nonOverlappingOpen`: No overlap allowed, open intervals
- `nonOverlappingClosed`: No overlap allowed, closed intervals

### 5.3 Bitemporal Model

For full bitemporal tracking (when enabled in definition):

```json
{
  "dateInterval": {         // Combined validity
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2050-12-31"
  },
  "validityInterval": {     // Business validity
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2050-12-31"
  },
  "transactionInterval": {  // System transaction validity
    "from": "2023-01-01T12:00:00.000Z",
    "to": "9999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"
  }
}
```

## 6. CRUD Operations

### 6.1 Create Entity Item

Basic create operation:

```json
// POST /entityItem/create
{
  "entityDefinitionCode": "accountHolder",
  "code": "ACC-001",
  "dateInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2050-12-31"
  },
  "name": "Acme Corporation",
  "type": "CORPORATE",
  "address": "123 Main St, Anytown, USA"
}
```

### 6.2 Read Entity Item

Get operation:

```json
// GET /entityItem/get
{
  "code": "ACC-001",
  "entityDefinitionCode": "accountHolder"
}
```

Load operation (with more details):

```json
// GET /entityItem/load
{
  "id": "012345678901234567890123",
  "entityDefinitionCode": "accountHolder"
}
```

### 6.3 Update Entity Item

Update operation:

```json
// POST /entityItem/update
{
  "id": "012345678901234567890123",
  "name": "Acme Global Corporation",
  "address": "456 Business Ave, Metropolis, USA"
}
```

### 6.4 Delete Entity Item

Delete operation:

```json
// POST /entityItem/delete
{
  "id": "012345678901234567890123"
}
```

## 7. Complete Examples

### 7.1 Simple Lookup Entity Item

For a country definition:

```json
{
  "entityDefinitionCode": "country",
  "code": "US",
  "dateInterval": {
    "from": "2000-01-01",
    "to": "2099-12-31"
  },
  "name": "United States"
}
```

### 7.2 Complex Entity Item with References

For an accountHolder definition:

```json
{
  "entityDefinitionCode": "accountHolder",
  "code": "ACME-CORP",
  "dateInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2050-12-31"
  },
  "name": "Acme Corporation",
  "type": "CORPORATE",
  "country": "US",
  "issuingBody": "00000000000000000000000000000001",
  "address": "123 Main St, Anytown, USA",
  "active": true,
  "establishedYear": 1985
}
```

### 7.3 Entity Item with Bitemporal Data

```json
{
  "entityDefinitionCode": "registryCertificate",
  "code": "CERT-123",
  "dateInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2023-12-31"
  },
  "validityInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01",
    "to": "2023-12-31"
  },
  "transactionInterval": {
    "from": "2023-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    "to": "9999-12-31T23:59:59.999Z"
  },
  "certificateType": "GREEN",
  "issueDate": "2023-01-01",
  "expiryDate": "2023-12-31",
  "status": "ACTIVE"
}
```

## 8. Interactive Creation Process

For creating a new entity item, follow these steps:

### 8.1 Basic Information
- Entity definition code (which definition to use)
- Item code (logical identifier)
- Validity period (dateInterval)

### 8.2 Required Attributes
- Identify all required attributes from the definition
- Provide values for each required attribute
- Ensure values match validation requirements

### 8.3 Optional Attributes
- Decide which optional attributes to include
- Provide values for included optional attributes
- Ensure values match validation requirements

### 8.4 References
- Identify references to other entities
- Ensure referenced entities exist
- Use correct reference keys (code, artifactId)

### 8.5 Temporal Validity
- Determine validity period
- Consider overlapping with existing items
- Set appropriate transaction and validity intervals if using bitemporal model

### 8.6 Special Attributes
- Handle artifact ID for authorization
- Add any system-specific attributes

### 8.7 Validation and Testing
- Verify all required fields are provided
- Check validation constraints
- Test CRUD operations
- Verify authorization works as expected
